{
  "name": "Challenging narratives through photography",
  "parent_id": 203313825234151,
  "group_id": "833d979a-50e3-49ca-9f3a-7f61c233ef1f",
  "alternates": [],
  "created_at": "2026-07-30T15:29:44.868Z",
  "deleted_at": null,
  "sort_by_date": null,
  "tag_list": [],
  "updated_at": "2026-08-12T15:10:31.605Z",
  "published_at": "2026-08-12T15:10:31.588Z",
  "id": 203717774852594,
  "uuid": "12db0aa9-9caa-4e7f-8ba2-bda351383566",
  "is_folder": false,
  "content": {
    "_uid": "e22f937d-1b86-feb3-da53-c8dc0e890e26",
    "body": [
      {
        "_uid": "3d2dc35e-3625-bcb0-63ab-45a0bb1dd9b8",
        "content": {
          "type": "doc",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "hard_break"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "We believe that tools like photography can be instrumental in gathering\ninsights, experiential accounts, and regular stories. But in a fast-paced,\nhyper-connected world where nearly anyone can capture a moment and post it\ninstantaneously to global audiences, we must also remember that with more\naccessibility comes the possibility of greater subjectivity. While being\nsubjective can have some positive implications, it may lead to harm if a visual\nrepresentation perpetuates prejudice, stereotypes, or an oversimplified story. ",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "The danger of single\npoint of view is often how photographs are described; although captions do\nprovide for context, they too can be misleading and depict an opinion that is\nnot always grounded in reality. This can happen when tourists visit low-income\ncountries. Sometimes what they post, from the\nphoto to the caption to the hashtags, can perpetuate stereotypes, rob the\nsubject of dignity or privacy, and further promote the idea that Western aid,\ncharity and intervention are the only solutions.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "It's\npossible that the golden age of charity\ncampaigns, that took place during the 1980s and 1990s, has paved the way for \"poverty porn\" and\nalso has directed the traditional \"African Narrative\" through writing, photography, film and other forms\nmedia. This has exploited people from a\nlower economic strata in order to generate the necessary sympathy that would\nlead to sales, donations, support, and in most cases today, likes on social\nmedia. What this fails to invite, however, is the question of why poverty\nexists in the first place, which can overgeneralise abject poverty in Africa without acknowledging its\nsociopolitical complexity and the individual contexts of the continent's 54 countries. ",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Since the satirical essay, \"",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "text": "How\nto Write about Africa",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "link",
                      "attrs": {
                        "href": "https://granta.com/how-to-write-about-africa/",
                        "uuid": null,
                        "anchor": null,
                        "custom": {},
                        "target": "_blank",
                        "linktype": "url"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": ",",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "underline"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "\" by\nKenyan Author, Binyavanga Wainaina, was published in 2006, many creatives in\nKenya, namely photographers, have followed in his footsteps of reclaiming their\nnarrative. Social media has\nespecially made it easier for them to publish their work, share it with a\nglobal audience, and challenge the (mis)representation of the African\nContinent. ",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "A great deal of visual storytelling is happening right in Nairobi, Kenya's capital. Nairobi - although an international hub\nfor international diplomacy, finance, banking and commerce - happens to be home\nto one of the largest slums in Africa: Kibera. Brian Otieno, born and raised in\nKibera, has been challenging perceptions about his neighbourhood through the\nfoundation of",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "text": "Kibera Stories",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "link",
                      "attrs": {
                        "href": "https://www.instagram.com/kiberastories/?source=post_page---------------------------",
                        "uuid": null,
                        "anchor": null,
                        "custom": {},
                        "target": "_blank",
                        "linktype": "url"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": ".",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "\"My project was different because the images were mixed\nup. It shows the good, the bad, and everything else. I started getting people\nthat were interested in knowing more about this place\". He explains, \"When you google Kibera, most of the images that will\npop up show the poverty side of Kibera\".\nOtieno's project goes beyond\nthe assumed appearance of the neighborhood to show its richness and diversity.\nHe documents its daily life, from street photography to hip-hop concerts,\nmarriages and social initiatives. ",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "This work does not come without obstacles, however. At a",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "text": "roundtable",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "link",
                      "attrs": {
                        "href": "https://witness.worldpressphoto.org/envisioning-the-future-of-photojournalism-in-east-africa-4b97b4107021",
                        "uuid": null,
                        "anchor": null,
                        "custom": {},
                        "target": "_blank",
                        "linktype": "url"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": " in\nNairobi about the 'Future\nof Photojournalism in East Africa',\nmany visual storytellers in attendance noted a lack of two factors that East\nAfrican photographers often face. ",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "type": "hard_break"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "ordered_list",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "list_item",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "text": "Education, which leads to an underestimation of the profession, and ",
                          "type": "text"
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "list_item",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "text": "Community, which prevents photographers from collectively working towards solutions. \"We don't gather, there are very few mentorships, little interactions and platforms. I see too much competition and no unity,\" argued Tony Karumba, Kenyan ",
                          "type": "text"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "text": " photojournalist and Photojournalism Workshop trainer, in a 2017 interview with ",
                          "type": "text"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "text": ".  ",
                          "type": "text"
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Thankfully another initiative, also in Kibera, is bringing those two\nthings to the future generation of storytellers. In late July of this year, an\nexhibition was held at the Alliance Française in Nairobi titled: \"Kibera Kids: A.I. - See the Future.\" On display at this event were many",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "text": "photographs",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "link",
                      "attrs": {
                        "href": "https://www.photostart.org/kibera-kids",
                        "uuid": null,
                        "anchor": null,
                        "custom": {},
                        "target": "_blank",
                        "linktype": "url"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": " captured by children from the area and later\nprocessed using artificial intelligence. The event was hosted by",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "text": "Photo Start",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "link",
                      "attrs": {
                        "href": "https://www.photostart.org/",
                        "uuid": null,
                        "anchor": null,
                        "custom": {},
                        "target": "_blank",
                        "linktype": "url"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": ", an organisation that offers photography classes to\nyoung students in lower-income areas like Kibera, and hopes to empower the\nfuture generation of visual storytellers through fostering creativity and\nmarketable skills.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "The caveat of breaking free from years of negative stereotypes while feeling the\nresponsibility to document what is actually happening has, at times, proven to\nbe a challenge for most visual storytellers. Nonetheless, it seems as though\nmost photographers agree that capturing something accurately and fairly\nsupersedes the fear of how it will be perceived by others. ",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "\"In countries that face endemic problems like Kenya,\nyou always find this problem,\"\nclaimed Karumba. \"People will think you are showing the country in a bad\nlight. If it is happening, it is happening and you have a responsibility to\ntell it as it is.\"",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Ultimately, a lot depends on the context. You need to consider the \"love thy neighbour as thyself\" philosophy. As an ethical philosophy, it\nrequires a photographer to treat their subjects as they would treat themselves.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Despite obstacles, photojournalistic initiatives developing in East\nAfrica hope to further challenge international views. It's clear that visual storytelling is best\ndone when it comes from a place of understanding an authenticity and, given the\nlocal and worldwide support of the work emerging out of East Africa so far, many\nare eagerly welcoming a more personal perspective. ",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "type": "hard_break"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "hard_break"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Note to readers: ",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "To view visual content from African photographers all over the\ncontinent, you can also look at the",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "text": "Everyday\nAfrica",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "link",
                      "attrs": {
                        "href": "https://www.instagram.com/everydayafrica/?hl=en",
                        "uuid": null,
                        "anchor": null,
                        "custom": {},
                        "target": "_blank",
                        "linktype": "url"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": " Instagram account. This social\nmedia campaign has created a new way of looking at foreign cultures, from a\nlocal perspective, to a global audience. To date, the account has roughly 5,051\nposts.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "hard_break"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Nina Plummer is an EAI Research Assistant, conducting research on\nmalnutrition, early childhood development, and learning outcomes.",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "italic"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "hard_break"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
